import FX1Poly.Core.GeneratorCore

/-! # Foundation/PolyCell/Core/GeneratorAdmission — admission ledger

This file ships the generator admission layer: `SupportedGenerator`,
an indexed inductive parameterized by `Generator` with exactly ONE
constructor per admitted generator (all 194, grouped by tier).

## The admission contract

Per `polycell.md` §4, the certified `PolyCell.gen` constructor takes a
`SupportedGenerator generator` parameter — admission is required to
introduce a typed term at the certified layer.  Three properties define
the admission discipline:

* **Closed enumeration:** every admitted generator gets exactly one
  constructor, named identically to the Generator constructor it admits.
  Adding a feature = adding ONE arm here (and an arm in each of the 7
  metadata tables).  No new `PolyCell` constructor.

* **Per-profile restrictability:** a restricted FX profile (e.g.,
  "no-HoTT FX", "constructive-only FX") re-defines this inductive
  with FEWER arms.  The default — `fxProfile` — admits all 194.
  Type-level evidence makes the admission set part of the soundness
  surface, not an invisible runtime check.

* **Cascade-reduction mechanism (the load-bearing claim):** adding a
  new feature costs:
  1. ONE `Generator` enum arm + 7 metadata table arms.
  2. ONE `SupportedGenerator` arm (this file).
  3. ONE `GenPayloadEvidence` arm when payload constraints differ.
  Total: ~9 lines per feature.  See `docs/potential_ctors.md` §0 for
  the cost analysis.

## Naming convention

Constructor names match the Generator constructors they admit
(`SupportedGenerator.gen_var` admits `Generator.gen_var`).  Lean's
namespacing disambiguates without conflict.  This preserves the
type-level 1:1 bijection between admission witnesses and admitted
generators — `cases` on a `SupportedGenerator g` value cleanly
dispatches per-feature when needed downstream.

## Zero-axiom discipline

No `deriving DecidableEq` — autogenerated DecEq for indexed inductives
can pull `propext` via `noConfusion` over the index mismatch cases.  The
admission witness type carries trivial DecEq anyway (one inhabitant per
generator, by index pinning).  If a downstream consumer needs DecEq, it
will be provided by hand via case-on-generator.

The 194-arm `cases g <;> exact ...` dispatch closes by structural
recursion on the closed enum — no propext, no Classical, no axiom. -/

namespace FX1Poly.Core

